## 4. Postcode → region mapping — how and where

The mapping is **duplicated in two places**, both as a hard-coded `Map<String,int[]>` named `REGION_POSTCODE_RANGES`:

1. `Owner.getRegion()` / `regionForPostcode()` (Owner.java lines 213-267) — used to DERIVE the region/locality on read. Precedence: 4-digit postcode range first (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), then a hard-coded city table (Sydney→NSW, Melbourne→VIC, Brisbane→QLD), else `"UNKNOWN"`.

2. `OwnerRestControllerV1.REGION_POSTCODE_RANGES` / `isPostcodeValidForCity()` (controller lines 267-299) — used to VALIDATE an incoming postcode against the region derived from the owner's city (via `owner.getRegion()`). Null postcode always accepted; present postcode must be 4 digits; if the city maps to a known region it must be in range; unknown region accepts any 4-digit value.

The same three ranges are also mirrored in the test harness (`AcceptanceBase.REGION_POSTCODES`) and documented in `openapi.yml` (postcode description ~lines 2037-2043, locality description ~lines 2181-2188). Note there is no separate "postcode-to-region lookup table/file" — it is inline code in these classes.

## Key mismatch to be aware of

The current production code generates `customerCode = "<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>"` (e.g. `SYD-SMI-0007`, per `generateCustomerCode` and documented that way in `openapi.yml` lines 2114-2122), and `membershipNumber = "<customerCode>-M<YY>"`. But the active acceptance test (`AcceptanceTest.coreCustomerCodeIsRegionHash`) and its file-level comment require the redesigned format `"<REGION>-<HASH8>"` matching `[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}`. So the test currently encodes the target design, not the implemented one — the implementation still needs to be changed to a region + 8-upper-hex-char SHA format.

